<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-a-nonuniform-rational-localisation">
  <title>A nonuniform rational localisation</title>
  <p>
    We next compute the rational chart which witnesses the failure of stable
uniformity.
  </p>
  <p>
    Consider the datum
<me>\alpha=(W;\varpi).</me>
The ideal <m>(\varpi,W)</m> is open, since <m>\varpi</m> is a unit in <m>A</m>.  Let
<me>A_\alpha=A\left\langle\frac{W}{\varpi}\right\rangle</me>
be the corresponding rational localisation on <m>|W|\leq |\varpi|</m>, and write
<m>X=W/\varpi</m>.  Since <m>\varpi</m> is already invertible in <m>A</m>, the algebraic
localisation does not change the ring.  More explicitly,
<m>A_\alpha</m> is obtained by taking the <m>\varpi</m>-adic
completion of <m>A_0[X]\subset A</m> and then inverting <m>\varpi</m>.
  </p>
  <proposition xml:id="prop-bad-chart">
    <statement>
      <p>
        There is a canonical isomorphism of complete topological rings
<me>A_\alpha\cong k\langle X,Q\rangle/(Q^2),
  \qquad W\longmapsto\varpi X,\quad Q\longmapsto Q.</me>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <lean project="AdicSpaces" commit="b007a4f3d4226f00a684b402715aa542e2f0bcdc" ref="FiniteJetOver.chartDatum_isRational">Lean (chartDatum_isRational)</lean>
    <lean project="AdicSpaces" commit="b007a4f3d4226f00a684b402715aa542e2f0bcdc" ref="FiniteJetOver.chartEquiv">Lean (chartEquiv)</lean>
    <lean project="AdicSpaces" commit="b007a4f3d4226f00a684b402715aa542e2f0bcdc" ref="FiniteJetOver.chartEquiv_continuous">Lean (chartEquiv_continuous)</lean>
    <lean project="AdicSpaces" commit="b007a4f3d4226f00a684b402715aa542e2f0bcdc" ref="FiniteJetOver.chartEquiv_symm_continuous">Lean (chartEquiv_symm_continuous)</lean>
    <lean project="AdicSpaces" commit="b007a4f3d4226f00a684b402715aa542e2f0bcdc" ref="FiniteJetOver.chartEquiv_canonicalMap_W">Lean (chartEquiv_canonicalMap_W)</lean>
    <proof>
      <p>
        Put
<me>T_0=k^\circ\langle X,Q\rangle/(Q^2),
  \qquad T=T_0[1/\varpi].</me>
Let <m>\rho:A\to A_\alpha</m> be the canonical map.  We first show that
        <men xml:id="eq-q2-killed-on-chart">Q^2C\subseteq\ker(\rho).</men>
        Take <m>y\in Q^2C</m>, and choose <m>r\geq0</m> such that
<m>\varpi^r y\in Q^2C_0</m>.  For every <m>n\geq0</m>, the coefficient description of
<m>A_0</m> gives
<me>W^{-n}\varpi^r y\in A_0:</me>
multiplication by <m>W^{-n}</m> only introduces negative powers of <m>W</m> in terms
of <m>Q</m>-degree at least two.  In <m>A_0[X]</m>, where <m>W=\varpi X</m>, we therefore
have
<me>\varpi^r y
   =W^n(W^{-n}\varpi^r y)
   =\varpi^nX^n(W^{-n}\varpi^r y)\in\varpi^nA_0[X]</me>
for every <m>n</m>.  Hence <m>\varpi^r y</m> maps to zero in
<m>\widehat{A_0[X]}</m>.  Since <m>\varpi</m> is invertible in
<m>A_\alpha</m>, this proves <xref ref="eq-q2-killed-on-chart"/>.
      </p>
      <p>
        We now construct the isomorphism.  Recall that the map
<m>\iota_B:A\to B</m> from the defining pullback is given by
<me>f_0(W)+Qf_1(W)+Q^2h\longmapsto f_0(W)+Qf_1(W);</me>
it simply discards the terms divisible by <m>Q^2</m>.  The substitution
<m>W\mapsto\varpi X</m>, <m>Q\mapsto Q</m> defines a continuous homomorphism
<m>B\to T</m>.  Their composite is
<me>\theta:A\longrightarrow T,\qquad
  f_0(W)+Qf_1(W)+Q^2h\longmapsto
  f_0(\varpi X)+Qf_1(\varpi X).</me>
It sends <m>A_0</m> into <m>T_0</m> and sends <m>W/\varpi</m> to <m>X</m>.  It therefore sends
<m>A_0[X]</m> into <m>T_0</m> and extends, first to the completions and then
after inverting <m>\varpi</m>, to a continuous homomorphism
<me>\Phi:A_\alpha\longrightarrow T.</me>
      </p>
      <p>
        Set
<me>\overline X=\rho(W)/\varpi,\qquad \overline Q=\rho(Q).</me>
Both elements are power-bounded: they belong to the completed ring of
definition coming from <m>A_0[X]</m>.  Moreover,
<m>\overline Q^{\,2}=0</m> by <xref ref="eq-q2-killed-on-chart"/>.  Evaluation at
<m>\overline X</m> therefore gives a continuous homomorphism
<me>\Psi:T\longrightarrow A_\alpha,\qquad
  f+Qg\longmapsto f(\overline X)+g(\overline X)\overline Q.</me>
      </p>
      <p>
        It remains to check the two composites.  Write
<m>a=f_0(W)+Qf_1(W)+y\in A</m>, with <m>y\in Q^2C</m>.  Then
<me>\Psi\Phi(\rho(a))
  =f_0(\varpi\overline X)+\overline Qf_1(\varpi\overline X)
  =\rho\bigl(f_0(W)+Qf_1(W)\bigr)
  =\rho(a),</me>
where the last equality uses <xref ref="eq-q2-killed-on-chart"/>.  The
image of <m>A</m> is dense in <m>A_\alpha</m>, so continuity gives
<m>\Psi\Phi=1</m>.  Conversely, <m>\Phi\Psi</m> fixes <m>k</m>, <m>X</m> and <m>Q</m>, and hence is
the identity on the dense subring <m>k[X,Q]/(Q^2)</m> of <m>T</m>.  It follows by
continuity that <m>\Phi\Psi=1</m>.  Thus <m>\Phi</m> and <m>\Psi</m> are inverse continuous
homomorphisms.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </proposition>
  <proposition xml:id="prop-not-stably-uniform">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The ring <m>A</m> is not stably uniform.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <lean project="AdicSpaces" commit="b007a4f3d4226f00a684b402715aa542e2f0bcdc" ref="FiniteJetOver.not_isUniform_chart">Lean (not_isUniform_chart)</lean>
    <lean project="AdicSpaces" commit="b007a4f3d4226f00a684b402715aa542e2f0bcdc" ref="FiniteJetOver.finiteJet_not_stablyUniform_of_dvr">Lean (finiteJet_not_stablyUniform_of_dvr)</lean>
    <proof>
      <p>
        In <m>T=k\langle X,Q\rangle/(Q^2)</m>, every element of the line <m>kQ</m> is nilpotent and hence power-bounded.  This line is not bounded with respect to the ring of definition <m>T_0</m>: for every <m>r\geq 0</m>,
<me>\varpi^r\bigl(\varpi^{-(r+1)}Q\bigr)=\varpi^{-1}Q\notin T_0.</me>
Thus <m>T</m> is not uniform.  By <xref ref="prop-bad-chart"/>, <m>A_\alpha</m> is not uniform.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </proposition>
</section>
